        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "62924793-e47f-55e4-a117-e2076f44beb0",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Products: Commodore 16, Commodore 116, Prológica CP-400\n- Companies: Commodore International, Prológica\n- Components: 7501 CPU, 8501 CPU\n- Places: Europe, Brazil\n- Concepts: entry-level home computers, 8-bit computer clones\n\nFacts:\n- In 1984, Commodore International released the Commodore 16 as a home computer intended to replace the VIC-20 at the entry level.\n- The Commodore 16 used a 6502-compatible 7501 or 8501 CPU.\n- A cost-reduced version of the Commodore 16, called the Commodore 116, was sold only in Europe.\n- In the middle of 1984, Prológica introduced the CP-400 to the Brazilian market as a new model in its \"CP\" personal computer series.\n- Prológica was a Brazilian company that made its own versions of 8-bit U.S. computers.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "374ee78b-0919-58f3-9a13-15ffda2a8899",
        "text": "Page: Commodore 16. Sentence: The Commodore 16 is a home computer made by Commodore International with a 6502-compatible 7501 or 8501 CPU, released in 1984 and intended to be an entry-level computer to replace the VIC-20.\nPage: Commodore 16. Sentence: A cost-reduced version, the Commodore 116, was sold only in Europe.\nPage: Prológica CP-400. Sentence: In the middle of 1984 a Brazilian company called Prológica, which made its own versions of 8 bits US computers, brought to the Brazilian market a new equipment for its personal computer series called \"CP\" (shorten of Personal Computer in Portuguese).",
